# TBIT-style probe: WSCALE option in a SYN-ACK that didn't have
# WSCALE in the original SYN MUST be ignored.
#
# RFC 7323 §2.2: window scaling is symmetric — both endpoints must
# offer the WSCALE option in their SYN / SYN-ACK for it to apply.
# A SYN-ACK that includes WSCALE without the SYN having offered it
# would be a protocol violation; the receiving stack should ignore
# the WSCALE field and treat the connection as unscaled.
#
# This script is the inverse: if we sent WSCALE in our SYN but the
# peer's SYN-ACK omits WSCALE, the resulting connection runs
# UNSCALED (snd_wnd interpreted at the on-wire face value, not
# shifted by the WSCALE we offered).
#
# Verifies: when the peer drops WSCALE, our `snd_wnd` interpretation
# stays at the raw 16-bit window field, so a subsequent data segment
# from us doesn't get capped by an inflated effective window.

--connect 10.0.0.1:49152 10.0.0.2:80
+0 > SEW 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460, wscale 5, sackOK, TS val * ecr 0>

# SYN-ACK omits WSCALE entirely — peer doesn't support / negotiate.
+.005 < S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 32768 <mss 1460, sackOK, TS val 1 ecr *>

# Our third ACK is a pure ACK with TS but no WSCALE (post-handshake
# segments never carry WSCALE per RFC 7323 §2.2). The advertised
# window field is OUR receive window — 65535 (raw), matching what we
# put in our SYN, because no WSCALE was negotiated.
+0 > . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 65535 <TS val * ecr 1>

# Now we send 4 KiB of data. With WSCALE NOT negotiated, snd_wnd ==
# peer's advertised window == 32768 (raw). Our four MSS-sized
# segments fit comfortably within that.
--send 4096
+0 > P. 1:1449(1448) ack 1 win 65535 <TS val * ecr 1>
+0 > P. 1449:2897(1448) ack 1 win 65535 <TS val * ecr 1>
+0 > P. 2897:4097(1200) ack 1 win 65535 <TS val * ecr 1>
